# The date this value was set, so "how long has this world stood" is a READ and not a
# walk of the log. Forty units landed in one night against this sha and nothing in the
# tree recorded that as evidence: each pull request checked the pin individually, forty
# times, and the aggregate — six of them touching src/ — was a fact nobody could state
# without reconstructing it from history (#1099). A stability claim that costs a
# `git log` is a claim nobody makes.
#
# It is a COMMENT because the payload rule is exactly one payload line, and that rule is
# read by two separate readers (lock 7 and tools/digest-move.sh); a second data line
# would make this file argue with itself. The grammar is
#
#     # sealed: <ISO date> <the commit that moved it> <where it was argued>
#
# and `tools/digest-move.sh --age` reads it. Moving the seal without moving this line is
# refused by the same script that refuses an undeclared move: a stamp nobody has to
# update is a stamp that is always wrong.
#
# `--selftest` runs the daemon twice and asks whether the two runs agree. Two runs of a
# weakened seal agree perfectly, so that question cannot catch a seal that got smaller
# (#898). This file is the outside witness: the sha that
#
#     java -cp out matrix.Main --headless --ticks 6000 --seed 42
#
# must print on its `DIGEST tick=6000` line. `.github/workflows/locks.yml` reads the value
# from here and from nowhere else.
#
# Exactly one payload line: <64 lowercase hex>  <why this value>. Blank lines and lines
# starting with `#` are ignored; a second payload line is refused rather than silently
# ignored, because a silently ignored pin is worse than no pin.
#
# A declared digest move (A10, D-020) is this one line, edited — the new sha and the reason
# it moved — in the commit that moves it. The move history is then
# `git log -p -- .github/canonical-digest`, kept by git, which cannot drift from the value
# it explains. Before this file existed that history could only be recovered by rebuilding
# old commits one at a time; #899 did exactly that, five times, to find out when the chain
# last moved.
#
# EDITING THIS LINE IS GATED (#884). Pasting a printed sha in here is the four-second way
# to turn a red build green, and it is the same keystroke whether the mover reasoned about
# the move or was tired of the red X. So the edit has a second obligation: the commit that
# makes it carries
#
#     Declared digest move: <old sha> -> <new sha>
#
# with both shas in full on one line and an issue number in the same message, and the
# reason field below names that issue too. `tools/digest-move.sh` is the reader, and it is
# lock 9 — it stays silent for every unit that does not move the seal, and refuses the
# ones that move it without saying so. The chain of heads is then one query:
#
#     git log --format=%B | grep '^Declared digest move:'

